figdown 0.1 bitfield
# `DECLARATION-ORDER-SEMANTICS`: the WORKED EXAMPLE for deriving bit numbers.
#
# Case 404 pins that the two numbering conventions differ in the model by
# exactly one key. This case pins what a reader is entitled to COMPUTE from
# that key, because the model itself carries no bit range: spec/core.md
# §12.7 licenses the derivation and states why the ranges are not
# materialized, and spec/genres/bitfield.md's semantic model is the
# arithmetic. Both blocks below declare the SAME field list in the SAME
# order, so the two models differ only in `numbering`, and the two
# DRAWINGS are geometrically identical — FIRST leftmost, SECOND to its
# right, under both values (`DECLARATION-ORDER-SEMANTICS`).
#
# Drawing position p_k = sum of the earlier widths (no `break` here, so it
# is also the declared offset); column c = p_k mod word; row = p_k / word.
#
#   field    width   p_k   columns
#   FIRST      4       0   0..3
#   SECOND    12       4   4..15
#
# Bit number of column c: msb0 -> c ; lsb0 -> word-1-c (word = 16).
#
#   block      field    bit numbers          note
#   reg_msb0   FIRST    0,1,2,3              ascending, left to right
#   reg_msb0   SECOND   4..15                ascending, left to right
#   reg_lsb0   FIRST    15,14,13,12          DESCENDING; highest bit at the left
#   reg_lsb0   SECOND   11..0                DESCENDING, left to right
#
# So under lsb0 the FIRST-declared field is bits 15..12, not bits 0..3 —
# and it is still the leftmost field drawn. A reader that reports 0..3 for
# reg_lsb0 has read the declaration order as the bit numbering; that is the
# misreading `DECLARATION-ORDER-SEMANTICS` exists to close.
bitfield reg_msb0 "Control (RFC convention)" word=16 numbering=msb0
field "FIRST" 4
field "SECOND" 12
bitfield reg_lsb0 "Control (register convention)" word=16 numbering=lsb0
field "FIRST" 4
field "SECOND" 12
